## Finding: one merged ticket is not one problem

The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) calls this one known parser issue with one
fix queued. Its own computed view separates a shared-parser problem from a booking-form
hint problem. Those need separate diagnoses and dispositions, not one shared fix.
The counts below come from the export dated 4 October 2026. They are not a live queue.

The follow-up engineering tickets are [the shared parser](https://github.com/pizang/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2)
and [the booking-v3 hint](https://github.com/pizang/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1).
The table below describes filing in the exported desk snapshot, before these GitHub
tickets were created.

## Engineering update and closure

The [course's live desk update](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows) reports
**#4471 closed 05 Oct**. It gives different outcomes for the two causes:

- **Parser — fixed:** `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. Close its engineering
  ticket as **completed**.
- **Hint — won't fix:** **booking-v3 form retired**. Close its engineering ticket as
  **not planned**, not completed: retirement removed the form, but did not fix its hint.

Closing both as fixed would erase that distinction. These are engineering's reported
outcomes, not an independent runtime verification of the product. The mechanism and
acceptance-check descriptions below explain the snapshot, not work still open.

## Counts and classification

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table counts each report ID once, using `uniqueById` from `causes.ts`, because export
pages overlap. It filters to reports merged into #4471 and groups with `causeOf`, resolving
the tenant through `locales.ts`. Tenant locale and booking-form version determine the
cause; neither the reporter's browser locale nor `meta.intakeTag` is authoritative.
The tag breakdown above exposes disagreements with intake instead of silently trusting it.

### Parser: the shared parser assumes month first

For day-first tenants, the shared parser interprets an intended day/month date as
month/day. App and email reports remain in this group even when a tenant runs booking-v3;
its form hint is not involved in those channels. Booking-v2 form reports also fall here.

Evidence: R-41200 describes 7 April becoming 4 July for `harbour.dental` (en-GB,
booking-v2, app). R-41203 describes 2 December becoming 12 February for `lantern.row`
(cs-CZ, booking-v2, app). R-41205 belongs here despite its intake `hint` tag and en-US
browser locale: `morrow.tax` is a fr-FR tenant and this is an app report.

Engineering should make date parsing respect the tenant's date order, then check both
ambiguous day/month inputs and unambiguous dates in affected channels. This is an
inference from the kb's classifier and reports, not a reproduction against product code:
the product implementation is not included in this export.

### Hint: booking-v3 tells day-first users to type month first

Booking-v3 displays `MM/DD/YYYY`, but the form interprets the entry in the tenant's
day-first order. This distinct path affects form reports from `alder.physio` (de-DE),
`copper.studio` (es-ES), and `pale.harbour` (pt-PT). A shared-parser fix alone does not
correct that instruction.

Evidence: R-41230 is a form report from `copper.studio` describing 6 January becoming
1 June, although intake tagged it `parser`. R-41241 is a form report from `alder.physio`
with an en-US browser locale; the tenant remains de-DE. R-41251 is a `pale.harbour` form
report describing 7 January becoming 1 July and occurs twice in the overlapping export.

Engineering should align the displayed hint with the tenant-specific interpretation and
check the displayed instruction and resulting date together for all affected tenants.
This mechanism comes from `DATE_HINT` and `causeOf`; the export does not include a live
booking form to exercise.

## Reports that the classification does not explain fully

`desk-notes.json` flags R-41202, R-41209, R-41236, R-41344, and R-41620 as read as a
one-day move. Their descriptions include adjacent-day dates, a June-only offset, an invite
arriving before booking, and different days in Lisbon and Toronto. The classifier places
all of them under `parser` by tenant and channel; the computed footnote above keeps them
in that count. This does not establish that a month/day parser explains those symptoms.

Keep those IDs visible on the parser ticket and ask for original timestamps, time zones,
and reproduction details before deciding whether they warrant another ticket. Do not
silently drop them, double-count them, or assert a time-zone root cause from these words
alone.

## The queue kept receiving reports

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The chart uses received and merged timestamps separately. The merge spike records desk
activity, not the start of the bug. Reports arrived before the mass merge and continued
through the end of the export. A known status, a queued fix, and a large merged ticket
are not evidence that the incoming problem stopped.

## Triage by cause, not by subject

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) and the
[triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) prescribe subject-only merging. Both were last reviewed
in June 2024; the shared layout marks them stale. An accepted historical decision is not
proof that it still matches the executable behavior. Leave that accepted record intact;
a new decision should replace it rather than rewriting its history.

<DedupeExplorer />

The explorer runs the exported rules. Different subjects can describe the same cause.
Matching subjects can hide different causes, including within one tenant across channels,
or between tenants on the same channel. Unknown tenants do not establish a shared cause.

<Callout>
Proposed replacement rule: identify the tenant, its locale, the form version, and the
report's channel; inspect the body and compare causes. Merge only when the cause is
established to be the same. If the tenant or cause is unknown, keep the reports separate
until investigated. A subject match is a search aid, not a merge decision.
</Callout>

The separate engineering tickets preserve each cause, count, anomaly note, and closure
reason. Recheck the live queue before applying these snapshot counts to new reports.
The original accepted decision and triage page still need an explicit replacement
by the desk; this review does not pretend that publishing a page changes its automation.

